Detailed list of the CHAOS I have introduced into the Linux kernel
==================================================================

OOM-killer process unkillability is swapped
-------------------------------------------

    If in the normal Linux kernel the OOM killer would be allowed to kill a
    process, it no longer can, and if it wouldn't in a normal kernel, it can now.
    Also, it targets processes using low memory first, and may target processes
    which wont release memory.

Randomness is NOT secure
------------------------

    All ChaCha permutations have 1 round only

Kernel Panic Blink Speed Increased
----------------------------------

    The kernel panic blink speed is up from once every 200ms to once every 100ms

Only one online CPU
-------------------

    The kernel always thinks there is one CPU online, and this is the boot CPU.

All SYSRQs are enabled
----------------------

    All SYSRQs are enabled all of the time.

Snowgrave
---------

    A new SYSRQ is registered for the 'N' key which sets all CPUs as inactive, not present, not possible, and inactive. Also sets the number of online CPUs to 0 and the number of possible CPUs to 0.

Processes are frozen permanently
--------------------------------

    Thawing a frozen process is a no-op.